      "technique": "Confocal Laser Scanning Microscopy (CLSM)",
      "why": "This inverted confocal microscope provides optical sectioning, which eliminates out-of-focus light to precisely map the 3D location of different fluorescent probes within a single cell.",
      "accessories_to_confirm": [
        "Laser lines matching the excitation spectra of your specific dyes",
        "High-magnification oil-immersion objective (e.g., 63x or 100x)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "instrument_id": "EQ08",
      "role": "primary",
      "technique": "Confocal Laser Scanning Microscopy (CLSM)",
      "why": "Like EQ07, this system offers high-resolution confocal imaging suitable for distinguishing membrane and nuclear compartments.",
      "accessories_to_confirm": [
        "Laser lines matching your dyes",
        "High-numerical aperture objectives"
      ]
    },
    {
      "instrument_id": "EQ09",
      "role": "supplementary",
      "technique": "High-content epifluorescence imaging",
      "why": "This system can capture fluorescent signals across many cells quickly, but as a widefield system, it may have more background blur than a confocal microscope when trying to resolve fine internal structures.",
      "accessories_to_confirm": [
        "Appropriate filter cubes for your dye wavelengths"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "not_available": [],
  "clarifying_question": null,
  "summary": "To precisely localize proteins within the cell, confocal microscopy is the best approach because it creates sharp 'optical slices' through the sample. Both EQ07 and EQ08 are high-end confocal systems capable of resolving the spatial difference between a nuclear and a membrane signal."
}

{
  "measurement_needs": [
    "gelation kinetics",
    "viscoelastic properties during crosslinking",
    "gel point determination"
  ],
  "objective_met": true,
  "recommendations": [
    {
      "instrument_id": "EQ19",
      "role": "primary",
      "technique": "Oscillatory Rheometry",
      "why": "The HR20 rheometer can perform time-sweep measurements to monitor the change in storage (G') and loss (G'') moduli as the hydrogel crosslinks, identifying the exact gelation point.",
      "accessories_to_confirm": [
        "UV-curing accessory (if using photo-initiators)",
        "Parallel plate geometry",
        "Disposable plates"
      ]
    },
    {
      "instrument_id": "EQ21",
      "role": "primary",
      "technique": "Oscillatory Rheometry",
      "why": "This hybrid rheometer is designed to measure the viscoelastic transition from a liquid precursor to a solid gel network.",
      "accessories_to_confirm": [
        "UV-curing accessory (if using photo-initiators)",
        "Parallel plate geometry",
        "Disposable plates"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "not_available": [
    {
      "technique": "UV-integrated Rheometry",
      "why_useful": "GelMA is frequently crosslinked using UV light; a rheometer with an integrated UV light source and transparent bottom plate allows for real-time monitoring of the reaction triggered by light."
    }
  ],
  "clarifying_question": null,
  "summary": "The transition from a liquid to a gel is best measured using oscillatory rheometry to track the material's viscoelastic properties over time. Both EQ19 and EQ21 are suitable for this measurement, though you will need to confirm if they have the specific UV-curing accessories typically required for GelMA photo-initiation."
}

{
  "measurement_needs": [
    "glass transition temperature (Tg)"
  ],
  "objective_met": true,
  "recommendations": [
    {
      "instrument_id": "EQ18",
      "role": "primary",
      "technique": "Differential Scanning Calorimetry (DSC)",
      "why": "DSC is the standard technique for determining the glass transition temperature of amorphous solid dispersions by measuring the change in heat capacity as the material transitions from a glassy to a rubbery state.",
      "accessories_to_confirm": [
        "Tzero pans and lids",
        "refrigerated cooling system (if Tg is expected to be near or below room temperature)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "instrument_id": "EQ21",
      "role": "primary",
      "technique": "Rheometry (Temperature Ramp)",
      "why": "The Environmental Test Chamber (ETC) allows for precise temperature control to measure the viscoelastic changes (drop in storage modulus) associated with the glass transition.",
      "accessories_to_confirm": [
        "Parallel plate geometry",
        "Solid torsion fixtures"
      ]
    },
    {
      "instrument_id": "EQ17",
      "role": "supplementary",
      "technique": "Thermogravimetric Analysis (TGA)",
      "why": "TGA should be used prior to DSC to determine the decomposition temperature and moisture content, ensuring the DSC scan range is safe and the Tg is not influenced by residual solvents.",
      "accessories_to_confirm": [
        "Platinum or alumina sample pans"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "not_available": [],
  "clarifying_question": null,
  "summary": "The glass transition temperature can be accurately measured using the DSC250 (EQ18), which is the standard method for pharmaceutical solid dispersions. Alternatively, the Discovery HR20 rheometer (EQ21) equipped with an Environmental Test Chamber can detect the transition through changes in mechanical stiffness. It is recommended to use the TGA (EQ17) first to identify the sample's thermal degradation limits."
}

{
  "measurement_needs": [
